<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">El-Kurru</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kurru</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">El-Kurru is a royal cemetery near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, burial place of the early kings of Kush. The tombs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashta">Kashta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shabaka">Shabaka</a>, Shebitku and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tantamani">Tantamani</a> lie there, with pyramids built in the Egyptian manner, and the kings' horses were buried nearby. It forms part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-barkal">Gebel Barkal</a> and the Sites of the Napatan Region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/el-kurru.jpg" alt="El-Kurru" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bertramz · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Kurru,main_pyramid.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>El-Kurru lies between the 3rd and 4th cataracts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, about 1.6 km west of the river, in what is now Northern state, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>. It was the first of the three royal cemeteries of the Kushite kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s 25th Dynasty; later rulers were buried at Nuri. The site is divided into three parts by two wadis. The central part, apparently the oldest, holds tumulus tombs, rock-cut pits under circular mounds of gravel and rubble like the burials of the C-Group and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a>, while rows of pyramids stand to the east and the queens' pyramids lie across the southern wadi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The cemetery was used mainly from about 860 to 650 BCE. George Reisner dated its earliest tomb to the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i">Sheshonq I</a>; some scholars, such as Hakem and Török, place the first burials at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twentieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twentieth Dynasty</a>, about 1070 BCE, while others, among them Kendall, broadly follow Reisner. The tombs grew from round mounds to square structures with offering chapels and then to pyramids. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a>'s tomb (Ku.17), the first with a known name, about 750 BCE, has a vaulted chamber both rock-cut and built of masonry, and the tombs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tantamani">Tantamani</a> and Queen Qalhata are painted with scenes of the Egyptian underworld.</p><p>About 120 metres from the queens' pyramids, four rows of graves held the horses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shabaka">Shabaka</a>, Shebitku and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tantamani">Tantamani</a>, buried upright with all their trappings. In the mid-4th century BCE an unnamed king built the largest pyramid of the site (Ku.1), which appears unfinished and unused. Almost every tomb was later robbed. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>, under the Christian kingdom of Makuria, a walled settlement stood here until about 1200, and Christian Nubians carved monograms, Christian symbols and many boats into pyramid Ku.1.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Reisner excavated the site in 1918 and 1919, and his assistant Dows Dunham published the report in 1950. Work resumed in 2013 under Geoff Emberling and Rachael J. Dann, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>'s National Council for Antiquities and Museums and the Qatar Sudan Archaeological Project.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “El-Kurru” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kushites built more pyramids, at <b>El-Kurru</b>, Nuri and Meroe, than were built in Egypt itself.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
